    "body": "Traffic congestion on U.S. highways cost the trucking industry an estimated $128.2 billion in 2024, according to a report released this week by the American Transportation Research Institute (ATRI) . It was the second-highest total the group has recorded and the third straight year the bill topped $120 billion.\nWhat’s Related\nThe national total fell from a record $129.8 billion in 2023. ATRI attributed the dip to lower truck mileage during the freight slump. Truck miles fell 1.5% in 2024, while average truck speeds on the congested freight routes ATRI tracked fell from 52.09 mph to 51.84 mph.\nTrucks lost a combined 1.24 billion hours to traffic in 2024. That’s the equivalent of about 452,000 truck drivers sitting idle for an entire working year, according to ATRI. The average cost came to $7,973 per registered truck.\nRELATED STORIES: Ranking The Top 10 U.S. Locations For Worst Truck Traffic | The Top 10 Issues Facing the Trucking Industry in 2026\nThe cost of time and fuel\nATRI used truck GPS data , federal highway figures, and its own trucking cost research to estimate the cost of delays. The group updated its hourly trucking cost calculation and applied the change to past years. On that basis, the 2024 total was $4.7 billion higher than in 2022 and nearly 29% above the pre-pandemic peak of $99.5 billion in 2018.\nATRI estimated that congestion caused trucks to burn 6.66 billion gallons of diesel in 2024, costing about $25 billion. Higher trucking costs and a return of highway traffic after the pandemic have kept the congestion bill near record levels, said Jeremy Carmichael, vice president of operations at Ruan Transportation Management Systems.\n“These costs can be addressed through targeted infrastructure investment in the nation’s most congested freight corridors, and a commitment at every level of government to upgrade our highways and improve supply chain efficiency,” Carmichael said.\nWhere costs were the highest\nTexas led all states at $10.92 billion, followed by California at $10.47 billion and Florida at $10.35 billion. Together, the 10 states with the highest costs accounted for 53% of the national total.\nThe New York City metro area ranked first at $7.49 billion, ahead of Miami at $3.69 billion and Chicago at $3.54 billion. ATRI used 2022 as the baseline for changes in state and metro costs. Over those two years, costs rose in 73 of the 107 metro areas studied. Charleston, South Carolina, had the largest percentage increase at 20.9%, which ATRI linked to rising port freight volumes and population growth.\nTruck traffic is outgrowing the interstates\nOver the decade through 2024, tractor-trailer mileage on interstates grew nearly 25%, while interstate lane mileage increased 4.5%. About 57% of tractor-trailer miles in 2024 were driven on interstates.\n“Investments like the Hunts Point Interstate Access Project in the Bronx are essential to improving freight mobility and supporting our region’s growth,” said Zach Miller, vice president of government affairs for the Trucking Association of New York. “We need sustained investment in infrastructure to reduce congestion and the costs it imposes on businesses and consumers.”",
